    q: 'A session-based sequence model (GRU4Rec-style) beats collaborative filtering for brand-new users, but underperforms CF for users with 50+ historical interactions. Why does the right architecture flip based on how much history a user has, and how would you design the system to handle both cohorts well?',
    answer: 'Session-based models only need the CURRENT session to make a recommendation, which is exactly why they excel at cold start — a brand-new user has no cross-session history for collaborative filtering to exploit, so a model that reasons purely over "what has this user done in the last few minutes" has a real advantage over CF, which has nothing to learn from for that user yet. But for a user with 50+ interactions spanning many sessions, collaborative filtering has access to a much richer, more STABLE signal: a latent preference representation built from long-term patterns across many sessions, capturing durable taste rather than just the current session\'s momentary intent. A pure session-based model, by design, can only see the current session and structurally cannot leverage that accumulated cross-session signal even though it exists in the logs. The right design isn\'t picking one architecture — it\'s routing by data availability: use the session-based model (or heavily weight it) for cold-start and low-history users, and progressively blend toward, or hand off to, the CF (or a hybrid sequence+CF) model as a user\'s interaction history accumulates, so each cohort is served by the architecture whose assumptions actually match how much signal is available about that user.',
    whatsTested: 'Whether you understand this as a data-availability tradeoff (what each architecture can and cannot see), not an architectural quality judgment ("one model is just better"), and can propose a routing/blending design rather than picking a single winner.',
    antiPattern: 'Declaring one architecture universally superior based on a single cohort\'s results. A model that wins for cold-start users and a model that wins for established users are solving genuinely different problems (single-session intent vs. long-term stable taste) — neither result generalizes to the other cohort.',
    staffFraming: 'A staff-level answer treats the blend threshold as a tunable design decision, not a hard cutoff: rather than a hard switch at some interaction count, a smoothly weighted ensemble (session-model weight decaying as history accumulates, CF weight rising) avoids a visible quality cliff at the threshold and lets the system be re-tuned as user behavior patterns shift, without requiring a full architectural rewrite.',
