    answer: 'Training-time diversity (A) makes every prediction diverse by construction — it\'s baked into what the model has learned to optimize — which sounds appealing but is actually inflexible: it applies the same diversity pressure uniformly, even in contexts where a user has made their intent to see MORE OF THE SAME thing extremely clear (five browsing actions on running shoes in a row, say), where injecting variety actively hurts the experience the user is asking for. It is also opaque and expensive to adjust: tuning the diversity/relevance tradeoff means retraining the model, and there is no simple dial to turn if the balance turns out wrong in production. Post-ranking diversity via MMR (B) is a controllable knob applied AFTER the relevance-optimized ranking already exists: it explicitly and interpretably trades off "how relevant is this candidate" against "how similar is it to items already selected for this list," with a tunable parameter governing the balance, and — critically — it can be adjusted, A/B tested, or reverted without retraining anything. This is why MMR-style re-ranking is the industry-standard approach: it keeps the core ranking model focused purely on relevance (simpler to train and reason about), and layers a separately tunable, interpretable, instantly-reversible diversity control on top, rather than entangling two different objectives inside one set of model weights.',
    whatsTested: 'Whether you can articulate the real tradeoff (inflexible-but-integrated vs. tunable-but-separate) and correctly identify post-ranking re-ranking as the industry default, with the reasoning (controllability, no retrain needed, context-dependent tuning) rather than just a preference.',
