    answer: 'The standard pattern is a weighted linear (or learned) combination of calibrated per-objective scores: value = w1·p(click) + w2·p(completion) + w3·p(share) − w4·p(churn-risk) + w5·(diversity term) + w6·(revenue term), where each pᵢ must first be well-CALIBRATED on its own scale (a raw uncalibrated head\'s score isn\'t comparable in magnitude to another head\'s, so blending uncalibrated scores lets whichever head happens to output larger numbers dominate the combination by accident, not by design). Diversity and revenue terms are qualitatively different from the engagement-prediction heads — they aren\'t "predictions" at all but explicit re-ranking adjustments (e.g., an MMR-style penalty for similarity to already-selected items, or a bid/margin term for a sponsored or higher-margin title) — so they are typically applied as a re-ranking layer on top of the blended engagement value, not trained end-to-end as one more head sharing the same network. The failure mode when weights are wrong is asymmetric and easy to get backwards: over-weighting short-term engagement heads (click, completion) relative to longer-horizon signals systematically favors sensational or binge-bait content that maximizes immediate engagement at the expense of the retention the platform actually needs; over-weighting revenue relative to relevance erodes trust and pushes users toward abandoning the platform entirely, which costs far more revenue long-term than it gains short-term. Because these effects only show up over weeks, not in a single day\'s metrics, weight-tuning has to be validated with long-horizon retention experiments, not a same-week A/B readout.',
