    answer: 'Sequential models earn their keep specifically when ORDER and recency within a session carry real predictive signal beyond a static preference profile — a user who just played three high-energy workout tracks in a row is very likely to want a fourth right now, a signal a static collaborative-filtering embedding (built from long-run aggregate taste) captures poorly, since it treats the user\'s overall profile as roughly stable rather than reacting to what just happened in the last few minutes. This makes sequential models a strong fit for session-continuation recommendations (queue/autoplay, "up next"), for catalogs with high sequential structure (episodic content, workout playlists, cooking-along videos where step order matters), and for users actively mid-session. Where it hurts: sequential models need a reasonably long, clean interaction sequence to have anything to condition on, so they are a poor primary architecture for cold-start users (a session-based model with a 1-item history has almost nothing to work with, and this is exactly the gap collaborative filtering\'s long-run stable preference profile fills instead), and they add real serving complexity and latency (maintaining and updating a live sequence representation per active session) that isn\'t justified for surfaces where users\' choices are driven mostly by stable long-term taste rather than momentary sequence (e.g., a "recommended albums for you" static browse page, where a session\'s last few plays are a weak, noisy signal compared to the user\'s established taste profile).',
