    level: 'Staff',
    q: 'Your two-tower retrieval model has a billion-row item embedding table that no longer fits comfortably in a single server\'s memory at your target serving latency. Walk through your options for serving it, and the tradeoffs between them.',
    answer: 'The item side of a two-tower model is precomputed offline, so the serving problem is purely "how do I look up and search a billion precomputed vectors fast," not "how do I run inference" — which opens several levers. (1) Sharding: partition the embedding table (and its ANN index) across multiple machines by item ID hash or by a semantically meaningful key (category, region), and fan a query out to relevant shards in parallel, merging top candidates — this scales linearly with machines but adds network fan-out latency and a merge step. (2) Quantization of the embeddings themselves (e.g., product quantization, as in IVF-PQ) trades a small amount of retrieval recall for a large memory reduction, letting more of the table fit on fewer machines or even in a single machine\'s memory — this is usually the first lever to pull since it\'s "free" scale-wise if the recall loss is acceptable. (3) Tiered serving: keep a smaller, frequently-accessed "hot" subset (popular or recently-active items) in fast in-memory storage, and a "cold" long-tail subset in slower but cheaper storage, accepting slightly higher latency on the rarer lookups — this exploits the fact that item popularity is almost always heavily skewed, so most traffic hits a small hot set. (4) Reducing embedding dimension directly (128→64) shrinks memory linearly but risks losing representational capacity — this needs an offline recall@k evaluation to confirm quality holds before shipping. In practice, most large systems combine several of these (sharding + quantization + hot/cold tiering) rather than picking one lever alone, because each one alone has a ceiling on how much memory it can reasonably reclaim.',
    whatsTested: 'Whether you can name multiple concrete, distinct scaling levers (sharding, quantization, hot/cold tiering, dimension reduction) with their real tradeoffs, rather than a single generic answer like "just use a bigger machine" or "shard it."',
    antiPattern: 'Treating "add more hardware" as the default answer without considering that quantization and hot/cold tiering can often reclaim an order of magnitude of memory for a much smaller recall/latency cost than horizontal sharding alone, which adds network fan-out complexity that a well-tuned single-lever fix might avoid entirely.',
    staffFraming: 'A staff-level answer frames this as a cost/recall Pareto decision made explicit and measured, not assumed: before committing to a scaling strategy, run an offline recall@k sweep across quantization levels and dimension choices to know exactly how much recall each memory-saving lever costs, then choose the cheapest combination that meets the recall floor the product actually needs — rather than over-engineering a fully-sharded, unquantized solution when a quantized single-shard approach might meet the bar for a fraction of the operational complexity.',
