    answer: 'Before writing off the improvement, I would check whether the extra items retrieval now surfaces are actually reaching the RANKER\'s effective top-k, or whether they\'re being retrieved but then buried by the ranking stage before a user ever sees them — an improvement in retrieval recall only translates to an engagement change if the newly-recalled items also rank competitively; if the ranker\'s features or training data don\'t discriminate well for this particular newly-recalled item type (say, they are systematically newer or less-established items with sparse historical engagement features), the ranker may just push them back down regardless of retrieval doing its job better. I would also check for a serving-layer bottleneck: if the ranking stage\'s candidate-set size (how many of the retrieved items it actually scores) didn\'t increase alongside retrieval\'s wider net, the extra recalled candidates may simply be truncated before ranking ever considers them — an unchanged downstream funnel width silently caps the benefit of a wider top-of-funnel. Finally, I\'d check whether the newly-recalled items, even when ranked and shown, are near-duplicates of what the old retrieval was already surfacing (overlapping candidate sets with only a few genuinely new items making it through) — in which case the offline recall@500 number improved on paper without meaningfully changing what a real user actually sees.',
